What enabling KEPLERIAN_LINK actually does, measured.

Sample:  4400 unnumbered objects drawn from UnnObs.txt, 400 in each of
eleven strata, chosen on the observations actually present rather than on
MPCORB's arc.  The hard strata are objects whose apparitions are far apart,
split by inclination.  fo run both ways on each, one working directory and
one config directory per run, settings stated explicitly in both arms.

Arc recovered (fraction of the available arc the final fit accounts for):

                                        base      ki
  2 apparitions >4yr apart, high incl   0.853   0.863
                            mid incl    0.802   0.812
                            low incl    0.690   0.697
  2 apparitions 1-4yr apart             0.907-0.973  unchanged
  3 apparitions >4yr apart              0.835-0.876  unchanged
  >=5 apparitions (control)             0.773   0.773
  ------------------------------------------------------
  mean                                  0.8488  0.8519
  recovered >=90%                         3262    3277
  recovered <10%                           343     330

Elements, against MPCORB:

                                        base      ki
  full-arc fits agreeing                  3240    3246
  full-arc fits disagreeing                 22      31

So enabling it recovers the full arc on 14 objects the ordinary methods
could not manage -- and eight of those fourteen come out wrong.  Net, it
buys about six additional correct long-arc fits at the cost of about nine
additional wrong ones.  That is not a good trade:  a confidently wrong
orbit is worse than an admitted failure.  KEPLERIAN_LINK is therefore off
by default, and should stay off until this is better.

The gain is real but small, and the errors are what limit it.  Note that
some fraction of MPC linkages are themselves wrong -- they are conservative
but not infallible -- so a few of the 31 disagreements may be cases where
the reference is at fault rather than the fit.  Not eight out of fourteen,
though.

Why it is not better is understood, and is not a fault in the linkage.  On
2015 HG96 the linkage bridges 1490 days where the sub-arc search manages
one day, and the orbit it produces is genuinely right:  node within 0.1
degrees of MPCORB, inclination within 0.12, argument of perihelion within
2.7, a within half a per cent.  But e is out by 0.017, and across a
baseline that long those errors leave residuals of thousands of arcseconds
-- far outside the range where the differential corrector's linearisation
means anything.  It never converges, and reports back essentially the seed
it was handed.  The linkage finds the right neighbourhood;  what is missing
is a way to refine a seed from there over a long arc.

Two candidate fixes were tried and neither worked:

  A scan over mean anomaly.  This looked compelling -- the mean anomaly is
  precisely the element the conservation laws leave free, which is why the
  compatibility vector carries an l component at all, and ten degrees of it
  would explain the residuals.  It does not:  scanning the full circle
  leaves the best residual at 3900 arcseconds, and over the whole sample
  the gains and the errors are unchanged, 33 and 23 either way.  The phase
  is not what is wrong.

  Handing the two ranges to herget_method() instead of handing over the
  linkage's state vector.  What the Keplerian integrals really produce is a
  pair of distances, and Herget builds a transfer orbit through both
  observed directions exactly, which should remove the shape error.  In
  practice herget_method() returned failure on every call and the fallback
  stood, leaving the result bit-for-bit unchanged.  This was not chased
  down.  max_herget_span() limits how long a transfer arc it will attempt,
  but for r ~ 2 au that limit is around 2300 days and the baseline here was
  1490, so the cause is elsewhere -- most likely get_real_arc() trimming an
  observation set that is mostly excluded.  This is the most promising
  thread left.
